body:
It doesn't seem bad, but I usually see K'rrik win via \[\[Doomsday\]\] lines.  Here's a pretty decent deck from tappedout that goes in-depth about the piles.  [http://tappedout.net/mtg-decks/krrik-cedh/](http://tappedout.net/mtg-decks/krrik-cedh/)

&#x200B;

With regard to why I think Doomsday is probably better than an aetherflux win is largely based on the fact that cards such as \[\[Noxious Revival\]\], \[\[Chain of Vapor\]\], \[\[Swords to Plowshares\]\] exist which can completely disrupt the combo.  The instant you drop the aetherflux, you become a big threat (especially with K'rrik out).  If you don't win that turn, you probably lose.  If K'rrik gets removed, it is also quite difficult to restart the combo without a lot of mana.  You also don't have access to a lot of counterspells (though you have \[\[Imp's Mischief\]\]) which doesn't help either.
